.TH ENV 1 "2026-08-10" "ragfarm" "ragfarm configuration"
.SH NAME
env \- how configuration reaches every part of ragfarm: .env, .env.example, .env.slotN, and the two virtualenvs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.RS
.nf
.I .env             \fRreal settings for THIS box. Gitignored. Never committed.
.I .env.example     \fRthe documented template. Git-tracked. Source of truth for
                 \fRwhat every variable means.
.I .env.slot0       \fRgenerated per vLLM slot. Git-tracked, do not edit.
.I .env.slot1
.fi
.RE
.SH DESCRIPTION
Read this before changing any setting, because the failure mode of getting it
wrong is almost never an error message. A variable set in the wrong file is not
rejected \- it is
.B ignored,
and the system runs on the default while you believe it is running on your
value.
.PP
There are two separate ideas that share a filename convention:
.TP
.B .env
The values in force on this machine. Contains credentials, so it is gitignored
and exists only on the host.
.TP
.B .env.example
The
.B documentation.
Git-tracked, every variable listed with commented-out defaults and the reasoning
behind them. This is the single source of truth for
.I what a variable does;
.I .env
is the source of truth for
.I what it is set to here.
When you add a variable, add it to
.I .env.example
in the same change, or the next person cannot discover it.
.SH HOW A VALUE REACHES ITS CONSUMER
Four different mechanisms, and they do
.B not
overlap. This is the part that has cost the most time historically.
.SS 1. Host services (systemd)
The unit files carry:
.RS
.nf
EnvironmentFile=-/home/dave/ragfarm/.env
.fi
.RE
.PP
The leading
.B -
means "optional". Templated units also read their per-slot file
.B after
the global one, so the slot file wins on any variable set in both.
.PP
Changing
.I .env
does nothing until the unit is restarted. Systemd reads it at start, not
continuously.
.SS 2. Containers (docker compose)
.I infra/compose.yaml
injects the whole file into each container:
.RS
.nf
env_file:
  - path: ../.env
    required: false
.fi
.RE
.PP
This is
.B not
the same as compose's own
.I .env
handling, and confusing the two is the classic mistake:
.IP \(bu 3
.B env_file
puts variables
.I inside
the container.
.IP \(bu 3
Compose's
.BI ${VAR}
substitution happens
.I in the compose file itself,
before any container exists, and takes its values from the
.B shell environment.
It never puts anything into a container by itself.
.PP
So a variable referenced as
.BI ${VAR:-default}
in
.I compose.yaml
needs to be in your
.B shell
when you run compose. That is what the loader is for:
.RS
.nf
source scripts/proxy-env.sh
docker compose -f infra/compose.yaml up -d
.fi
.RE
.PP
Skip the
.B source
and compose silently substitutes the default. Anything already set under a
service's
.B environment:
key beats the file.
.SS 3. Scripts and tools
Python code should read settings through
.I ragfarm_env.py
at the repo root rather than calling
.B os.environ
with a hand-typed name \- that is what keeps spellings consistent and reports
deprecated ones.
.RS
.nf
\&.venv/bin/python ragfarm_env.py     # print everything as resolved, with warnings
.fi
.RE
.SS 4. Per-slot generated files
.IR .env.slot<N> " is written by "
.BR activate_llm (1)
and carries only what differs
.B between
slots: model path, served name, port, memory share, and whether the slot gets a
reasoning parser. It has a do-not-edit header and is rewritten on every
activation, deleted on
.BR --clear .
.PP
It is git-tracked on purpose: it is the record of what a slot was actually
serving, and the diff of a model swap is a useful review. Edit the registry and
re-activate; never edit these by hand.
.SH THE TWO VIRTUALENVS
There is no
.IR .venv0 / .venv1 ,
and there is no per-slot Python environment. There are exactly two, and they are
kept apart deliberately:
.TP
.I .venv
The project environment: the ingester, the services, the scripts in
.IR scripts/ ,
Open WebUI setup, the benchmarks. Pinned around torch cu130, numpy 1.26 and
transformers 4.57.
.TP
.I .venv-vllm
vLLM only. Both slots are served by this interpreter; the unit template puts it
first on
.BR PATH .
.PP
They are separate because installing vLLM into
.I .venv
downgrades torch off the cu130 build and moves numpy to 2.x and transformers to
5.x, underneath a frozen ingester. Two environments cost disk; one environment
costs a working corpus pipeline.
.PP
Consequence for anything you run by hand: use the right interpreter.
.I .venv/bin/python
for scripts,
.I .venv-vllm/bin/vllm
for the server. Neither is on
.B PATH
by default, which is intentional \- an ambiguous
.B python
is how the wrong one gets used.
.SH PRECEDENCE
Highest wins:
.IP 1. 4
A variable already exported in the invoking shell.
.IP 2. 4
A service's inline
.B environment:
block in
.IR compose.yaml ,
or a unit's
.B Environment=
line.
.IP 3. 4
.IR .env.slot<N> ,
for the templated vLLM units only.
.IP 4. 4
.IR .env .
.IP 5. 4
The default baked into the code, or the
.BI ${VAR:-default}
fallback in
.IR compose.yaml .
.SH CHANGING A SETTING
The full procedure, because a partial one is how settings appear not to work:
.IP 1. 4
Read the entry in
.I .env.example
first. It says which consumer the variable reaches and often why the current
value is what it is.
.IP 2. 4
Edit
.IR .env .
.IP 3. 4
Restart what consumes it. Host services:
.BR "sudo systemctl restart <unit>" .
Containers:
.B source scripts/proxy-env.sh
then
.BR "docker compose -f infra/compose.yaml up -d <service>" .
A compose
.B restart
is
.B not
enough for a changed environment \- it reuses the existing container. You need
.B up -d
so it is recreated.
.IP 4. 4
Verify from the running system, not from the file:
.RS
.nf
\&.venv/bin/python ragfarm_env.py
systemctl show -p Environment <unit>
docker exec <container> printenv | grep <VAR>
.fi
.RE
.SH THINGS THAT ARE NOT IN .env ON PURPOSE
.TP
.B Per-checkpoint facts
Whether a model needs
.B --reasoning-parser
depends on the checkpoint, not the box.
.B LLM_REASONING_PARSER
used to be global, and on 2026-08-09 an Instruct slot inherited the value meant
for the Thinking models: the parser keys on
.IR </think> ,
found none, and filed the model's
.B entire answer
as reasoning. The chat message came back empty with the whole reply inside the
collapsed thinking trace \- indistinguishable, at a glance, from a broken quant.
It is now
.B LLM_REASONING_ARGS
in
.IR .env.slot<N> ,
written from the registry's
.B profile
field. A global cannot express a per-checkpoint fact.
.TP
.B Anything the registry owns
Model paths, aliases and memory shares are derived from
.I models/llm/active.json
by
.BR activate_llm (1).
Setting them by hand in
.I .env
puts you one model swap away from a stale value.
.TP
.B Secrets in git
.I .env
is gitignored and must stay so. Put the
.B name
of a new secret in
.I .env.example
with an empty or obviously-fake value; never the value.
.SH REFERENCE: VARIABLES WORTH KNOWING
Not exhaustive \-
.I .env.example
carries the full inventory with reasoning. These are the ones that are load
bearing and unlikely to change.
.TP
.B RAGFARM_PUBLIC_HOST
The address a
.B remote browser
uses to reach this box. Everything else in the system is server-to-server and
correctly says 127.0.0.1; this one is fetched by the user's browser, so
loopback would mean
.I their
machine. Two consumers must agree on it:
.I setup_openwebui.py
bakes it into the draw.io wrapper, and
.I compose.yaml
puts it in
.B IFRAME_CSP
so the preview iframe is allowed to load from it. The script autodetects when
unset and warns; compose
.B cannot
autodetect, so leaving it unset gives you a correct URL that the CSP then
blocks. Set it.
.TP
.B OWUI_HOST
.B 0.0.0.0
(default) makes Open WebUI reachable from the LAN, login-gated. It is the only
service meant to be externally reachable; restrict :3000 at the host firewall
regardless. Set 127.0.0.1 for loopback-only and reach it over an SSH tunnel.
.TP
.B LLM_MAX_MODEL_LEN
Context window per slot, currently 32768. It is the hard wall that
.B max_tokens
lives under: prompt plus completion must fit. Raising it costs KV cache, which
is charged against the same memory budget
.BR activate_llm (1)
polices.
.TP
.B LLM_MOE_BACKEND
NVFP4 MoE kernel.
.B auto
is recommended \- it re-evaluates what is actually available, which changes with
the JIT cache state. Plain
.B flashinfer
is
.B not
a valid value and argparse rejects it.
.B marlin
is the last resort: it dequantises FP4 to BF16 and forfeits the speedup.
.TP
.B LLM_GPU_MEM_UTIL
Present in
.I .env.example
for documentation, but in practice
.B .env.slotN
supplies it per slot and that wins. Unified memory has no private VRAM, so
vLLM's 0.9 default would reserve most of the box and starve everything else.
.TP
.B MAX_JOBS
Bounds parallel CUDA compiles during a build. Unset, ninja runs
.I nproc+2
jobs and the OOM killer takes the service at ~98 GB. A different budget from
.B --gpu-memory-utilization
entirely \- lowering the vLLM memory flags does nothing for it.
.TP
.B RAGFARM_KV_GIB
Not read from
.IR .env :
it is an override you put in front of one
.BR activate_llm (1)
invocation to give that slot a larger KV target.
.TP
.B HTTP_PROXY " / " HTTPS_PROXY " / " NO_PROXY
Normalised by
.IR scripts/proxy-env.sh ,
which is why network-touching commands are prefixed with
.BR "source scripts/proxy-env.sh" .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I .env
Real values. Gitignored.
.TP
.I .env.example
Documented template, git-tracked. The full variable inventory lives here.
.TP
.I .env.slot0 ", " .env.slot1
Generated by
.BR activate_llm (1).
.TP
.I ragfarm_env.py
Canonical loader and resolver; run it to see what the code actually sees.
.TP
.I scripts/proxy-env.sh
Sourced, not executed. Loads
.I .env
into the shell so compose substitution and network commands work.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR activate_llm (1),
.BR fetch_llm (1),
.BR active.json (1),
.BR stack (1),
.BR setup_openwebui (1)
.PP
.I docs/deployment.md
for the running system;
.I infra/compose.yaml
for the container plane;
.I CLAUDE.md
for the build protocol.
